-- The vector type - Design-Vector.md §2, Implementation-Vector.md stages 1-3.
--
-- What this fixture can assert is the *type* and nothing above it: there are no `Num` instances over
-- a vector and no way to build one from source until stage 9's library lands, so a vector here is a
-- value that arrives, travels and leaves. That is deliberately not nothing - it is the whole of the
-- ABI contract Design-Vector §2.5 states, and it is the half of it a target could break silently.
--
-- Three things are visible in the goldens:
--
--  * `Vec(Float)` and `Vec(Float, 4)` are one type on a target whose natural width is four lanes, so
--    `natural` and `explicit` have the same signature. The printed form is `Vec(Float)` for both,
--    which is §1.4's printing rule: a diagnostic and a golden must not become target-specific.
--  * A vector is direct, so it is an argument and a result rather than a place - there is no
--    `alloca` and no `load` anywhere below.
--  * `Mask(Float)` and `Mask(I32)` intern to one type, since a mask's identity is its lane width and
--    lane count (§2.4) - so `maskLanes` and `maskFloat` have one signature between them.

fn natural(v: Vec(Float)) -> Vec(Float) = v

fn explicit(v: Vec(Float, 4)) -> Vec(Float, 4) = v

-- Half the natural count of a `Float` at any width this compiles for, so this is the one that
-- prints its count - and the one that says the explicit form is a type of its own rather than a
-- spelling that collapses.
fn half(v: Vec(Float, 2)) -> Vec(Float, 2) = v

fn bytes(v: Vec(U8)) -> Vec(U8) = v

fn maskFloat(m: Mask(Float)) -> Mask(Float) = m

fn maskLanes(m: Mask(I32)) -> Mask(I32) = m

-- A `@bits` refinement is a lane and carries its own range into the lanes - §2.2. It occupies the
-- storage a standalone value of it does, so `Vec(Count)` has the shape `Vec(U32)` has.
alias Count = @bits(30) U32

fn refined(v: Vec(Count)) -> Vec(Count) = v

-- Two vectors in one signature, which is what says the calling convention has more than one vector
-- register to hand out.
fn second(a: Vec(Float), b: Vec(Float)) -> Vec(Float) = b

-- A vector inside a record is storage rather than a register, which is the other half of the Repr:
-- sixteen bytes at alignment sixteen, and no fields of its own for a place walk to reach into.
data Wrapped {tag: Int, lanes: Vec(Float)}

fn unwrap(w: Wrapped) -> Vec(Float) = w.lanes

fn tagOf(w: Wrapped) -> Int = w.tag
